PG.notes("l010", "<p class=\"summary\">Short announcement article: the bootcamp, first made in 2018, was re-recorded through 2023, so most of the material is new. It names the new topics and explains the move from MongoDB to PostgreSQL.</p>\n<h3>What changed in the 2023 rebuild</h3>\n<ul><li>Around 80% of the course was re-recorded, with more than 50 hours of fresh video added.</li><li>Whole new sections were created for CSS Flexbox, CSS Grid and PostgreSQL.</li><li>The database part of the course switched from MongoDB (NoSQL) to PostgreSQL (SQL), based on hiring research showing employers want SQL skills more than Mongo/NoSQL.</li><li>Takeaway for study planning: older forum answers, blog posts or GitHub repos may refer to the 2018-era version (e.g. Mongo/Mongoose lessons), so check that any outside material matches the current section order.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Mixing up course versions: tutorials or repos labelled with older course years may follow the old MongoDB track instead of the current Postgres one.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l011", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains the role of HTML among the three front-end file types, then unpacks the name Hypertext Markup Language: hypertext means linked documents, markup means tags that label parts of the content. Sets up the heading tag as the first element to learn.</p>\n<h3>Where HTML fits in a website</h3>\n<ul><li>Every browser (Chrome, Safari, Brave, etc.) does the same job: it reads HTML, CSS and JavaScript files and renders them as a web page.</li><li>Modern sites combine all three, but only HTML can stand on its own. A CSS file alone or a JavaScript file alone does not make a page; an HTML file alone does.</li><li>The earliest websites were nothing but HTML.</li><li>HTML's job is the content and structure of the page. Example: a file containing just a heading element reading 'My Website' opens in the browser as that heading and nothing else.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Rough division of labour used for the rest of the course: HTML = structure/content, CSS = appearance, JavaScript = behaviour.</li></ul>\n<h3>Hypertext</h3>\n<ul><li>Hypertext is text that links to another document. Links (hyperlinks) are the core mechanism the whole web of HTML pages is built on.</li><li>The first website, built by Tim Berners-Lee, is mostly blue hyperlinks. Clicking one (e.g. from Project.html) loads a different HTML file. Navigating a site is just moving between linked HTML documents.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web (HTML, HTTP, URLs) rather than the Internet itself, which is the underlying network. The first site is still online at info.cern.ch.</li></ul>\n<h3>Markup language</h3>\n<ul><li>Markup means adding symbols around content to say what that content is, rather than adding content itself. Quotation marks in English are a familiar example: they tell the reader a passage is a quote.</li><li>Another comparison is an editor annotating a manuscript with marks for bold, underline and so on.</li><li>HTML does its markup with tags: keywords inside angle brackets, wrapped around the content they label.</li><li>Early HTML had only a few tags. Today there are many, but day-to-day work uses a small core set, such as headings h1 to h6 and the paragraph tag p.</li><li>No need to memorise the full tag list. The course introduces each tag when a project or exercise needs it, and MDN's element reference lists the rest.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The smallest useful HTML file: one heading, which the browser renders on its own with no CSS or JS</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Markup in practice: tags label what each piece of text is; the a tag is the hypertext part</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;about.html&quot;&gt;About me&lt;/a&gt;  &lt;!-- a hyperlink to another HTML document --&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking a site can be built from CSS or JavaScript alone: there must always be an HTML document for them to attach to.</li><li>Trying to learn every HTML tag up front instead of learning them as they come up.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Confusing the Web (documents linked by hypertext) with the Internet (the network it runs on).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l012", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows where each lesson's downloadable starter files live on Udemy and how to unzip them on Mac and Windows before opening them in VS Code.</p>\n<h3>Getting the files</h3>\n<ul><li>Lessons with starter material have a Resources dropdown (folder icon) in the Udemy player. Open it and click the link; most downloads are a single .zip archive.</li><li>A .zip is a compressed archive. Its contents (code files plus assets such as images and sounds) cannot be used until they are extracted.</li></ul>\n<h3>Extracting the archive</h3>\n<ul><li>Mac: double-click the .zip in Downloads; Finder creates a normal folder next to it with the contents.</li><li>Windows: select the .zip in File Explorer and use the 'Extract all' button in the toolbar, or right-click the archive and choose 'Extract all'. Note the destination folder it extracts to.</li></ul>\n<h3>Opening in VS Code</h3>\n<ul><li>Drag the extracted folder (not the .zip) onto the VS Code window. VS Code opens it as a workspace with every file listed in the Explorer.</li><li>Dragging the compressed archive in may seem to work, especially on Windows where files can sometimes be previewed, but editing and saving will misbehave later.</li><li>Rule of thumb: any download ending in .zip gets extracted first, every time.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Command-line alternative for extracting and opening a lesson folder (macOS/Linux; needs the VS Code 'code' shell command installed)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd ~/Downloads\nunzip &quot;2.1+Heading+Element.zip&quot; -d &quot;$HOME/Web Development Projects&quot;\ncode &quot;$HOME/Web Development Projects&quot;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Opening the still-compressed .zip in VS Code: files may show but cannot be worked on reliably.</li><li>Forgetting where Windows extracted the folder and then editing a different copy.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s02-headings", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the h1–h6 heading elements, the difference between a tag and an element, and why headings describe a page's hierarchy. Ends with an exercise that turns a flat line of text into a book outline using h1–h4.</p>\n<h3>Tags vs elements</h3>\n<ul><li>A tag is anything wrapped in angle brackets. &lt;h1&gt; is the opening tag; &lt;/h1&gt; is the closing tag. The only difference between them is the forward slash after the &lt;.</li><li>An element is the whole unit: opening tag + content + closing tag. So &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt; is one h1 element whose content is the text 'Hello World'.</li><li>The two terms get used loosely online, but the distinction matters when reading docs: 'tag' means the markup, 'element' means the thing in the page.</li></ul>\n<h3>What headings are for: hierarchy, not size</h3>\n<ul><li>The idea is borrowed from a book's table of contents: a top-level title, chapters under it, sections under chapters, sub-sections under sections.</li><li>HTML gives exactly six levels: h1 (most important) to h6 (least). There is no h7. Anything below h6 in importance should use a different element (paragraphs etc., covered later).</li><li>Browsers style headings by default so h1 is the largest and h6 the smallest. That default styling is only a visual hint of the level; CSS can change the look later without changing the meaning.</li><li>Opening and closing tags must match: &lt;h1&gt;…&lt;/h6&gt; is invalid. Each level uses the same number in both tags.</li></ul>\n<h3>Heading best practices (conventions, not errors)</h3>\n<ul><li>Use only one h1 per page. Think of it as the book title. Every other heading goes underneath it as h2, h3, and so on.</li><li>Don't skip levels. If a page has an h3, it should also have an h2 above it. Go h1 → h2 → h3 in order as you need deeper levels.</li><li>Breaking these rules won't break the page visually. They're professional conventions, and they matter for accessibility and search engines, which read the heading outline.</li></ul>\n<h3>Workflow introduced in this lecture</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the lesson's starting files from the Udemy 'Resources' button (zip named after the lesson, e.g. '2.1 Heading Element').</li><li>Unzip/extract the download first, then move the extracted folder into a single 'Web Development Projects' folder kept somewhere permanent. Unextracted zips are the most common reason the folder won't open properly in VS Code on Windows.</li><li>In VS Code: File → New Window → open the Web Development Projects folder in the Explorer.</li><li>Each exercise folder has index.html (where you write code), solution.html (check only after attempting), and a goal image showing the target result.</li><li>Preview with the Live Preview extension: right-click index.html → Show Preview. If it's missing, the VS Code setup lessons were skipped.</li><li>Saving (Cmd/Ctrl+S) may auto-format the file and change indentation. That's normal and doesn't affect the result.</li><li>MDN Web Docs is the reference to check whenever you meet an unfamiliar element. Don't read it cover to cover; look things up as you need them.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Anatomy of a heading element</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- opening tag   content     closing tag --&gt;\n&lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">All six heading levels (default browser sizes shrink from h1 to h6)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;Heading 1&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;h2&gt;Heading 2&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;h3&gt;Heading 3&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;h4&gt;Heading 4&lt;/h4&gt;\n&lt;h5&gt;Heading 5&lt;/h5&gt;\n&lt;h6&gt;Heading 6&lt;/h6&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 2.1 Heading Element: book outline</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html contains the words Book, Chapter 1–3, Section 1/2 and Diagram 1 as plain text. In the preview they run together on one unformatted line with no hierarchy.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Mark up the text with heading elements so the preview matches the goal image: a clear book → chapter → section → diagram outline.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Wrap 'Book' in a single h1 (the page title).</li><li>Wrap each of 'Chapter 1', 'Chapter 2', 'Chapter 3' in h2.</li><li>Wrap each 'Section N' inside the chapters in h3.</li><li>Wrap 'Diagram 1', which belongs to Section 1 of Chapter 2, in h4. It's the deepest level used.</li><li>Save, check Live Preview against the goal image, then compare with solution.html.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Four visibly different heading sizes in the order h1, h2, h3, (h4), with one h1 and no skipped levels.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Book&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;Chapter 1&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Section 1&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Section 2&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;Chapter 2&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Section 1&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;h4&gt;Diagram 1&lt;/h4&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;Chapter 3&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Section 1&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Section 2&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mismatched numbers in opening/closing tags (&lt;h2&gt;…&lt;/h3&gt;).</li><li>Using more than one h1, or picking a heading level for its size rather than its place in the outline.</li><li>Jumping from h1 straight to h3.</li><li>Trying to open the still-zipped download in VS Code.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s02-paragraphs", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the p element, shows why unmarked text collapses into one run-on block, and walks through an exercise that splits three blocks of Lorem Ipsum into separate paragraphs. Closes with where placeholder text comes from and how to generate it.</p>\n<h3>The paragraph element</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax mirrors the heading element: an opening tag &lt;p&gt;, the paragraph's text as content, then a closing tag &lt;/p&gt;.</li><li>Without p tags, plain text typed into the HTML file renders as one continuous run: separate blocks of writing flow into each other on the same line, and the reader can't see where one paragraph stops and the next starts.</li><li>Wrapping each block in its own p element makes the browser display each one as a separate block, with a blank gap between them, so paragraphs are visually distinct.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The browser ignores the line breaks and blank lines typed in the source. Any run of whitespace collapses to a single space, which is why pressing Enter in the editor does nothing to the layout and tags are needed.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The gap between paragraphs comes from the browser's default stylesheet (roughly 1em margin above and below each p). CSS can change it later.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> p is a block-level element: it starts on a new line and takes the full available width.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why it matters: accessibility</h3>\n<ul><li>Screen readers used by blind or low-vision visitors detect where each p element begins.</li><li>This lets the user jump from one paragraph to the next instead of listening through the whole page, so correct markup is about structure, not only looks.</li></ul>\n<h3>Lorem Ipsum placeholder text</h3>\n<ul><li>Lorem Ipsum is dummy text used in web and print design to simulate real copy while the actual content is not yet written.</li><li>Repeating a phrase like 'this is a paragraph' over and over is a poor substitute: identical words and identical sentence lengths don't look like natural text, so the layout preview is misleading.</li><li>The text derives from a work by Cicero (over 2000 years old) and has been used by printers since the 1500s to fill page layouts.</li><li>lipsum.com generates any number of paragraphs, words, bytes or lists at the click of 'Generate', and offers versions in other languages (e.g. Dutch, Polish).</li><li>Novelty generators exist for fun: baconipsum.com (meat words), broipsum.com (slang), and many more found by searching for 'funny lorem ipsum' (Vegan Ipsum, Pirate Ipsum, etc.).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In VS Code, Emmet expands the abbreviation lorem (or lorem50 for 50 words) followed by Tab into placeholder text without leaving the editor.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Without p tags the two blocks render on one continuous line</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  This is the first block of text.\n  This is the second block of text.\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;!-- renders as: This is the first block of text. This is the second block of text. --&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">With p tags each block is its own paragraph, separated by a gap</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;This is the first block of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;This is the second block of text.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Emmet shortcut in VS Code: type this, then press Tab</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">p&gt;lorem30\n&lt;!-- expands to &lt;p&gt;…30 words of Lorem Ipsum…&lt;/p&gt; --&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 2.2 Paragraph Element: separate three paragraphs</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download and extract the '2.2 Paragraph Element' zip from the lesson resources, move it into the Web Development Projects folder and open it in VS Code. index.html holds three blocks of Lorem Ipsum labelled 'First paragraph.', 'Second paragraph.' and 'Third paragraph.' with no tags. Right-click index.html, choose Show Preview, and the three blocks appear as one long jumble.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Use the p element so the preview shows three clearly separated paragraphs, matching goal.png.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open index.html (the file to edit; solution.html is only for checking afterwards).</li><li>Place an opening &lt;p&gt; immediately before the words 'First paragraph.'</li><li>Place the closing &lt;/p&gt; right after the last word of that first block. Moving the tag by click-and-drag or by cut and paste both work; only the final position matters.</li><li>Repeat for the second block: &lt;p&gt; before 'Second paragraph.', &lt;/p&gt; after its final word.</li><li>Repeat for the third block.</li><li>Watch Live Preview update: each block should now sit on its own with a blank line between them.</li><li>Compare against goal.png, then open solution.html to confirm the tags are in the same places.</li><li>Optional extra challenge: generate your own placeholder text (Lorem Ipsum, Bacon Ipsum, Bro Ipsum...) and build a short article out of several p elements in the same index.html.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Three paragraph blocks of default-size body text, each starting on a new line with an empty line of space between them, as in goal.png. No headings or other elements.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Paragraph Element&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;\n    First paragraph. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit,\n    sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. ...\n  &lt;/p&gt;\n\n  &lt;p&gt;\n    Second paragraph. Suscipit adipiscing bibendum est ultricies. Tortor\n    aliquam nulla facilisi cras fermentum. ...\n  &lt;/p&gt;\n\n  &lt;p&gt;\n    Third paragraph. Nisl purus in mollis nunc sed id semper risus. Ipsum a\n    arcu cursus vitae congue mauris rhoncus aenean. ...\n  &lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Expecting blank lines or Enter presses in the source to create separate paragraphs; only the p tags do that.</li><li>Putting the closing &lt;/p&gt; in the wrong spot (e.g. after the next paragraph's first sentence), which merges or splits blocks incorrectly.</li><li>Forgetting the closing tag. (Extra) Browsers often auto-close a p when the next block element starts, so it may look fine while the structure is wrong.</li><li>Using repeated identical filler text instead of Lorem Ipsum, which hides how real text will wrap.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's index.html and solution.html both include a personal 'Made with ... by Harshit Soni' footer element that is not part of the course; ignore it. Its index.html is already solved, so it does not show the untagged starting state.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s02-void-elements", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces void (self-closing) elements, focusing on the horizontal rule &lt;hr /&gt; and line break &lt;br /&gt;: how their single-tag syntax differs from a closing tag, when to use each, and why br must not replace p. The exercise formats a William Blake profile page with h1, p, br and hr, and the solution adds a diff-checking tip for finding bugs.</p>\n<h3>Void vs non-void elements</h3>\n<ul><li>Non-void elements (h1-h6, p, ...) have an opening tag, content in between, and a closing tag.</li><li>A void element is one that is not allowed to contain any content. Because there's nothing to wrap, it is written as a single tag: no separate opening and closing tags.</li><li>The name of the element goes first, then a space (by convention), then a forward slash, then the closing angle bracket: &lt;hr /&gt; and &lt;br /&gt;.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Other void elements met later in the course include &lt;img /&gt;, &lt;input /&gt;, &lt;meta /&gt; and &lt;link /&gt;.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where the slash goes (and which slash)</h3>\n<ul><li>In a closing tag such as &lt;/p&gt;, the slash comes right after the opening angle bracket, before the tag name.</li><li>In a void element such as &lt;br /&gt;, the slash comes after the tag name, just before the final angle bracket. Easy to mix up, so check the position.</li><li>It must be a forward slash (/, leaning right), never a backslash (\\, leaning left).</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;hr /&gt;: horizontal rule</h3>\n<ul><li>Renders a horizontal line across the page at the point where it appears in the document.</li><li>Use it to divide content and signal that what follows is a separate, unrelated part, e.g. placing it between two paragraphs puts a line between them.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Semantically, hr marks a thematic break in content; its look (thin grey inset line by default) can be restyled with CSS.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;br /&gt;: line break</h3>\n<ul><li>Forces the following text onto a new line without starting a new paragraph.</li><li>Meant for text that is still one paragraph but whose line divisions carry meaning: poems (line breaks control rhythm, rhyme and where to pause) and postal addresses.</li><li>Example used: the opening four lines of Blake's 'Auguries of Innocence'. Written inside a single p without br they run together into one line; with a br at the end of each line they display as proper verse.</li><li>The br itself holds no content; it only marks where the break goes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Do's and don'ts</h3>\n<ul><li>Don't use br to fake new paragraphs (stacking breaks to push text down). It harms accessibility: screen-reader users rely on real p elements to understand and navigate content.</li><li>Create a new p for every real paragraph; reserve br for line breaks inside one paragraph (poem, address).</li><li>Both &lt;br /&gt; and &lt;br&gt; (and &lt;hr /&gt; and &lt;hr&gt;) are valid HTML. In HTML5 the trailing slash is ignored: the parser already knows br and hr are void after reading the tag name.</li><li>Recommended style in the course: keep the trailing slash, because it makes void elements easy to spot when reading code and avoids hunting for a closing tag that doesn't exist. Expect to see the slash-less form online too.</li></ul>\n<h3>Debugging tip: diff your code against the solution</h3>\n<ul><li>Copy solution.html into one side of diffchecker.com and your index.html into the other, then click 'Find Difference'. Every mismatch gets highlighted.</li><li>Harmless differences also show up, e.g. an extra space between 'Blake' and &lt;/h1&gt; changes nothing in the rendered page.</li><li>Real bugs stand out: if the closing tag is mistyped (e.g. the h is dropped so it reads &lt;/1&gt;), the h1 never closes and everything after it renders as giant heading text. The diff points straight at the broken tag.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">hr between two paragraphs draws a dividing line</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;p&gt;First paragraph about one topic.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Second paragraph about something unrelated.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Closing tag vs void element: note where the slash sits</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;/p&gt;    &lt;!-- closing tag: slash BEFORE the name --&gt;\n&lt;br /&gt;  &lt;!-- void element: slash AFTER the name, before &gt; --&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">A poem inside one paragraph, with br at each line end (Blake, public domain)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;p&gt;\n  To see a World in a Grain of Sand&lt;br /&gt;\n  And a Heaven in a Wild Flower,&lt;br /&gt;\n  Hold Infinity in the palm of your hand&lt;br /&gt;\n  And Eternity in an hour.\n&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Do / don't: real paragraphs vs stacked breaks</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- Don't: br used to fake paragraphs --&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Paragraph one text.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Paragraph two text.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Do: one p per paragraph --&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Paragraph one text.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Paragraph two text.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Both spellings are valid HTML5; the course prefers the slash</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;br /&gt;  &lt;hr /&gt;   &lt;!-- preferred in the course --&gt;\n&lt;br&gt;    &lt;hr&gt;     &lt;!-- also valid --&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The bug shown with the diff tool: an unclosed h1 swallows the rest of the page</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;William Blake&lt;/1&gt;   &lt;!-- typo: should be &lt;/h1&gt; --&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;17 south molton street &lt;br /&gt; ...&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;!-- everything below now renders at h1 size --&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 2.3 Void Elements: William Blake profile</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download and extract the lesson's starter folder and open index.html. It contains plain, unformatted text: the name William Blake, his London address on four lines (17 south molton street / London / W1K 5QT / UK), and two Wikipedia paragraphs about him. In the preview it is one jumbled block.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Format the page with everything learned so far (h1, p, br, hr) so the preview matches goal.png.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Wrap 'William Blake' in an h1: it's the most important text on the page.</li><li>Wrap the four address lines together in one p (an address is a single paragraph).</li><li>Add &lt;br /&gt; at the end of each address line so each part sits on its own line within that paragraph. Typing it once and copy-pasting to the other lines is fine.</li><li>Wrap the first Wikipedia block in its own p, and the second block in another p, so the biography shows as two separate paragraphs.</li><li>Insert &lt;hr /&gt; between the address paragraph and the first biography paragraph, separating the name/address from the description.</li><li>Preview and compare with goal.png, then compare with solution.html. Optionally paste both into diffchecker.com to spot any differences.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Large 'William Blake' heading; the address on four stacked lines with no gaps between them; a grey horizontal line; then two separate biography paragraphs with a gap between them.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;William Blake&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;\n    17 south molton street &lt;br /&gt;\n    London &lt;br /&gt;\n    W1K 5QT &lt;br /&gt;\n    UK &lt;br /&gt;\n  &lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;hr /&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;\n    William Blake (28 November 1757 – 12 August 1827) was an English poet,\n    painter, and printmaker. ... (first Wikipedia paragraph)\n  &lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;\n    Although Blake was considered mad by contemporaries for his idiosyncratic\n    views, ... (second Wikipedia paragraph)\n  &lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing the slash in the closing-tag position (&lt;/br&gt;) or using a backslash (&lt;br \\&gt;).</li><li>Trying to put content inside a void element, or adding a closing tag such as &lt;/hr&gt;.</li><li>Using several br elements to create space between paragraphs instead of separate p elements (bad for screen readers).</li><li>Splitting the address into four separate p elements: it is one paragraph broken into lines.</li><li>A typo in a closing tag (e.g. &lt;/1&gt;) leaves an element open and restyles everything after it; diff against the solution to find it quickly.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's files include a personal 'Made with ... by Harshit Soni' footer that is not part of the course; leave it out. Its index.html is already completed, so it doesn't show the unformatted starter.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s02-movie-ranking", "<p class=\"summary\">End-of-section project: build a one-page 'favourite movies' site using only the elements from Section 2 (h1, h2, hr, h3, p). The brief sets a minimum structure of a title, subtitle, divider and at least three films, each with a short description; the walkthrough builds a sample version.</p>\n<h3>Purpose of the project</h3>\n<ul><li>Combines everything from the section into one page; no new elements are introduced.</li><li>Inspiration: film critic sites such as rogerebert.com, which list reviews and ratings. A personal list of favourites is handy when someone asks for a recommendation.</li><li>Scope is up to the learner: favourites of all time, of the past year, or any other theme.</li></ul>\n<h3>Required structure (minimum spec)</h3>\n<ul><li>h1: the site title (e.g. 'The Best Movies According to &lt;your name&gt;'). One per page, as the top of the outline.</li><li>h2: a subtitle directly beneath it (e.g. 'My top 3 movies of all-time.').</li><li>hr: a void element that separates the title block from the movie list.</li><li>h3: one per film title, at least three films. h3 is used because each film sits beneath the h2 in the heading hierarchy, so no level is skipped.</li><li>p: under each film's h3, a paragraph saying why you like it.</li><li>Every one of these elements must appear at least once; beyond that the content is free.</li><li>The wording typed in the video walkthrough differs slightly from goal.png (title and subtitle phrasing); only the element structure is checked, so the text should be your own.</li></ul>\n<h3>How the elements map to the layout</h3>\n<ul><li>The browser's default heading sizes (h1 &gt; h2 &gt; h3) give the page a visual hierarchy with no CSS at all: big title, smaller subtitle, then medium film names.</li><li>The hr draws a full-width grey line under the subtitle.</li><li>Each p renders as normal body text with a gap above and below, so each film's description is visually tied to the heading above it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Ranking order here comes purely from source order. A later section introduces ordered lists (ol), which would number the ranking automatically.</li></ul>\n<h3>Going further (optional)</h3>\n<ul><li>Add more to the descriptions, extra films, or br elements, e.g. a short poem about a favourite movie with a br at each line end.</li><li>Share a screenshot of the finished page in the course Q&amp;A to swap recommendations with other students.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Skeleton of the required structure</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;Site title&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;h2&gt;Subtitle&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;Movie 1&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Why I like it.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;Movie 2&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Why I like it.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;h3&gt;Movie 3&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Why I like it.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Optional extension: a short poem under a film using br inside one p</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h3&gt;Drive&lt;/h3&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;\n  Neon on the freeway,&lt;br /&gt;\n  a quiet man, a fast car,&lt;br /&gt;\n  a synth score that hums all night.\n&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 2.4 Movie Ranking Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download the 'Movie Ranking Project' zip from the lesson resources, extract it, and open the folder in VS Code. It contains index.html (a basic HTML page to fill in), solution.html (a sample answer, scrolled down) and goal.png (the target look).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Build a page listing at least three favourite films with a title, subtitle, divider, a heading per film and a paragraph explaining each choice, matching the structure in goal.png (with your own text).</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Inside body, add an h1 with the site title, e.g. 'The Best Movies According to &lt;your name&gt;'.</li><li>Add an h2 underneath as a subtitle, e.g. 'My top 3 movies of all-time.'</li><li>Add &lt;hr /&gt; to separate the heading block from the list.</li><li>Add an h3 with the name of your first film.</li><li>Under it, add a p with a sentence or two about why you like it.</li><li>Repeat the h3 + p pair for at least two more films (in ranked order).</li><li>Preview with Live Preview and check that all five element types appear and the hierarchy looks like goal.png.</li><li>Optional: extend the page (longer descriptions, more films, br-formatted poem) and post a screenshot in the Q&amp;A.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A plain, unstyled page: large bold title, slightly smaller bold subtitle, a horizontal grey line, then three medium-size bold film names, each followed by a normal-weight description paragraph. goal.png shows Spirited Away, Ex Machina and Drive.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Movie Ranking Project&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;The Best Movies According to Angela&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;My top 3 movies of all-time.&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;hr /&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Spirited Away&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;This is my favourite anime. I love the beautiful images.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Ex Machina&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Really cool sci-fi movie.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Drive&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Super beautiful film. Really artistic.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using h2 (or h4) for the film titles: films sit under the subtitle, so h3 keeps the outline in order without skipping a level.</li><li>Adding a second h1 for each film instead of one h1 for the whole page.</li><li>Writing &lt;hr&gt;&lt;/hr&gt; or putting text inside the hr; it is a void element.</li><li>Leaving out one of the required elements (often the hr or the description paragraphs).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo divergence: the student's files say 'According to Wesley', replace the first film title with the student's own name, include the typo 'favotite', and add a personal 'Made with ... by Harshit Soni' footer. The solution above follows goal.png instead.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l017", "<p class=\"summary\">Short study-habits video at the end of the first module: write down why you are learning to code using the goal-setting worksheet in the next lesson, and keep it visible for when the material gets harder.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Starting is already progress: many people who buy online courses never begin.</li><li>Expect the difficulty to rise later in the course; plan for motivation dips in advance.</li><li>Use the goal-setting worksheet (next lesson) to answer: why learn to code, what you want to achieve with the skill, and what life looks like once you have it.</li><li>Print the completed sheet and keep it where you study, so you can re-read your reasons when progress stalls.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Goal-setting worksheet</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The worksheet PDF attached to the following lesson (the reference repo also stores a copy as '02.05 - Goal Setting Questions.pdf').</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Record personal reasons and goals for learning web development.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the worksheet from the next lesson's resources.</li><li>Answer each question about your motivation and the outcome you want.</li><li>Print it and place it somewhere visible from your study desk.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A filled-in sheet of goals on display while working through the course.</p>\n</div>");
